---
/**
 * "Start here" — a path for people who are early.
 *
 * A founder with no in-house IT, or a developer who wants to understand what
 * they are being sold, has the same problem: nobody explains this in plain
 * language. Research on 2026 sites is clear that a beginner who reads
 * something useful comes back when the problem becomes urgent. So this section
 * exists to be useful first and to sell second.
 */
const steps = [
  {
    n: '01',
    title: 'What actually runs your software',
    meta: '3 min read',
    body: 'The smallest set of things a product needs to be online: a place to run code, a database, a way to serve requests, and DNS pointing at it. Nothing else is required to start.',
  },
  {
    n: '02',
    title: 'Why releases feel scary',
    meta: '4 min read',
    body: 'Almost always the same cause: nobody can say what changed, who changed it, or how to undo it. A pipeline with review, tests and a rollback path is the whole fix.',
  },
  {
    n: '03',
    title: 'Backups are not a plan until you restore one',
    meta: '4 min read',
    body: 'The number that matters is not how often you back up, it is how long a restore takes and whether anyone has done it. A tested restore is evidence; a green tick is not.',
  },
  {
    n: '04',
    title: 'What "SOC 2" and "ISO 27001" actually ask of you',
    meta: '6 min read',
    body: 'Both are mostly about evidence: proving the controls you claim are real and in place. The engineering work is smaller than the paperwork suggests — if you keep the right records.',
  },
  {
    n: '05',
    title: 'Reading your cloud bill',
    meta: '5 min read',
    body: 'Cost is a graph, not a number. The first three questions to ask are: what changed, what is idle, and what are we paying for at peak that we only need at peak.',
  },
  {
    n: '06',
    title: 'Access: the control that matters most',
    meta: '4 min read',
    body: 'Shared logins, no MFA, and a person who left with access still enabled. These three cause more confirmed breaches than any exotic attack, and all three are cheap to fix.',
  },
];
---

<section class="section" id="start" aria-labelledby="start-title">
  <div class="wrap start-here">
    <div>
      <p class="eyebrow">Start here</p>
      <h2 id="start-title">New to this? These six explain it plainly.</h2>
      <p class="intro" style="margin-top:16px">
        Most of our work starts with someone who does not yet know what to ask for.
        These are the explanations we would give on a call anyway — written down, free,
        and written to be useful on their own.
      </p>
      <ol style="margin-top:24px;padding-left:1.25em;display:grid;gap:8px">
        <li style="color:var(--ink-2);font-size:16px">You do not need to know the right answer before you ask the question.</li>
        <li style="color:var(--ink-2);font-size:16px">We will tell you if the fix is something your team should own instead.</li>
        <li style="color:var(--ink-2);font-size:16px">Nothing here is a sales pitch. It is the same material we use internally.</li>
      </ol>
    </div>
    <div class="start-panel">
      <span class="label">What to read first</span>
      <p>
        If you only read one, read <b>03</b>. Nearly every serious incident we have
        reviewed traces back to a backup that had never been restored.
      </p>
      <p>
        If you are about to sign your first enterprise contract, read <b>04</b> — it is
        the document your customer's security team will ask you for.
      </p>
      <p style="margin-top:18px">
        <a class="link" href="/insights/">See all guides <span aria-hidden="true">→</span></a>
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="wrap" style="margin-top:clamp(32px,4vw,52px)">
    <div class="path-list">
      {steps.map(s => (
        <a class="path-step" href="/insights/">
          <span class="path-num">{s.n}</span>
          <div>
            <h3>{s.title}</h3>
            <p>{s.body}</p>
          </div>
          <span class="path-meta">{s.meta} →</span>
        </a>
      ))}
    </div>
  </div>
</section>
